# My Claude & Dev Learnings

A running log of lessons from building with Claude — meant to carry over across projects, not just this one.

---

## 1. Working with Claude

- **Split work into separate chats by purpose.** Coach chat (guidance/sequencing only), requirements chat (spec updates only), build chats (one per feature). Mixing purposes in one chat burns quota and gets confusing.
- **One feature per build chat.** Never ask for the whole app at once. Small, testable steps.
- **Start each build chat by loading current code from GitHub**, not by re-explaining the whole app from scratch.
- **Ask for only new/changed code**, not full re-prints of unchanged files — saves quota.
- **Watch chat length.** Once a chat is getting long, start fresh — as long as key docs (requirements, build sequence) are current, no history is lost.
- **"Add from GitHub" connector can fail intermittently.** Retry once, then fall back to manually pasting the file content rather than burning quota troubleshooting it.
- **"Add to project" vs. GitHub sync:** the quick "Add to project" button has no version history and isn't tied to the real repo. Committing to GitHub + using "Sync now" in project knowledge keeps one true version with history — worth the extra clicks for docs you'll keep revising.
- **GitHub repo-sync setup is browser/desktop only** for initial configuration — not available in the Android app. Set it up once on desktop/browser; using it afterward may still work on mobile.

## 2. Dev Workflow

- **Test after each small step**, rather than batching multiple features before debugging.
- **Catch structural/architectural issues early.** If something feels off (a UI pattern, a missing layout piece), fix it or insert a new build step before building more on top of it — cheaper than retrofitting later.
- **Re-test old features after big structural changes.** A step that touches a lot of the file (e.g. a layout restructure) can silently break earlier work — don't just check the new feature.
- **Split large files once they get big.** A single-file app gets expensive to rebuild every step past ~1,000+ lines, since every build chat has to resend/retype the whole thing even for small changes. Splitting into e.g. index.html / style.css / app.js means most future chats only touch the one file that changed.
- **One-file-per-type (html/css/js) isn't the end state — it just delays the problem.** In this project, index.html and style.css stayed small, but app.js kept growing since all logic lands in one place. For future projects, plan a further split *within* the logic layer from early on — e.g. separate modules by feature/responsibility (books.js, shelves.js, importExport.js, api.js, ui.js) rather than one growing app.js. Assess this at the requirements/architecture stage, not after the file is already large.

## 3. Project Patterns (reusable architecture decisions)

- **Identify API key needs at the planning stage, not mid-build.** Before starting a new project, check which features will call external APIs (book search, maps, weather, etc.) and whether they need a key. This shapes architecture decisions early (client-only vs. needing a backend) instead of surfacing as a surprise later.
- **Public repos are fine for GitHub Pages apps** — a private repo gains no real privacy since the live site exposes the source anyway.
- **Client-side API keys are exposed in page source — know the scope tradeoff.** Restricting by HTTP referrer and using a free tier with no billing attached reduces the risk, but the key is still visible to anyone who looks. This is only acceptable for a test/personal/limited-scope project. For anything with real users or a paid tier, a key needs a backend (even a small serverless function) to stay private.
- **No-backend apps have real limits**: no login, data lives in each browser's own localStorage (lost on clearing data or switching devices) until a cloud sync step is built. Worth stating this plainly early so it's an accepted tradeoff, not a surprise later.
- **Identify API key needs at requirements stage, not mid-build.** Early on, ask: will this feature need an external API? Does that API require a key? Knowing this upfront shapes architecture decisions (backend vs. client-only) before code is written around the wrong assumption.
- **A private API key exposed client-side (visible in page source) is only acceptable for a test/limited-scope project** — not for anything real-world/production. If a project is going to scale or handle real users, plan for a backend or serverless proxy to keep keys server-side instead.

## 4. Mistakes & Fixes

- **What happened:** Didn't ask upfront for logic to be built in small modules by functionality. Result: app.js grew into one large file holding everything.
  **What I do now:** At project kickoff, explicitly request a file/module structure organized by functionality (e.g. books, shelves, import/export, API calls, UI) — not just by file type (html/css/js) — so no single file becomes a catch-all.

- **What happened:** No test strategy was defined at the start. Testing was purely "does it feel right" (user experience), with no repeatable steps.
  **What I do now:** Define a lightweight test strategy early — a running list of test steps/checks per feature (e.g. "add book → appears in correct shelf → survives page reload") that gets reused and expanded as the project grows, instead of relying on memory/feel each time.

- **What happened:** UX/UI wasn't designed upfront — requirements described features and fields, but not layout. The mismatch only became clear at step 7 (layout restructure), meaning earlier steps were partly built against the wrong shape.
  **What I do now:** Treat UX/UI as its own requirements step, before or alongside early build steps — not something discovered mid-build. Options to try next time: sketch/describe screen layouts in the requirements doc itself, or use a mockup tool (e.g. a wireframing tool, or even asking Claude to generate an HTML/CSS mockup or a text-based wireframe) to visualize layout early, then translate that into structured requirements before writing app logic.

## 5. Tools Not Yet Explored

*(Things worth trying/comparing in future projects, not yet evaluated.)*

- **GitHub Copilot** — haven't tried building directly in GitHub with it. Current workflow is Claude (chat-based) + manual paste into GitHub's browser editor. Worth comparing later: where Copilot's in-editor suggestions might complement or replace parts of this workflow, and for what kind of tasks.
- **AI mockup/wireframe tools** — for planning UX/UI before build (see lesson above). Options to try:
  - **Gemini** — can produce text/ASCII wireframes plus layout specs (colors, typography, components) from a plain-language prompt describing the screen and flow; can also generate rough visual mockup images, though quality drops for complex interfaces. Likely most useful as a fast, free first pass to turn into requirements text.
  - **Dedicated wireframing tools** (e.g. Figma, Banani) — more polished/editable canvas if a quick prompt-based mockup isn't precise enough.

## 5. Tools to Explore / Compare

- **GitHub Copilot** — not yet tried. Current workflow is Claude (chat) generating full files → manual paste into GitHub's browser editor. Copilot works differently (inline suggestions directly in an editor, incl. GitHub.com's own editor and VS Code). Worth testing on a future project to compare: speed, quota/cost, and whether it suits a no-local-dev-environment workflow as well as the current approach does.

---

## Quota Tips (quick reference)

- Keep build chats short and single-feature.
- Ask for only new/changed code.
- Test after each step instead of batching.
- Start a new chat once a chat is getting long, rather than pushing through.
